<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Protectorate General to Pacify the East</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/668">668</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Andong Protectorate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> General to Pacify the East was set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> in 668, after the fall of Goguryeo, to rule the conquered lands of Goguryeo and Baekje. Its existence led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silla-tang-war">Silla–Tang War</a>, and in 676 the Tang moved it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. It was later reduced in status and eventually abolished in the eighth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/protectorate-general-to-pacify-the-east.jpg" alt="Protectorate General to Pacify the East" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lệ Xuân · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_China_under_Emperor_Gaozong.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, known in Chinese as the Andong Duhufu, was created beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ungjin-commandery">Ungjin Commandery</a> in the former Baekje lands, and was said to have been garrisoned with 200,000 soldiers. It was meant to oversee nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a>, 42 prefectures and 100 counties holding an estimated 697,000 Goguryeo households. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-tang">Emperor Gaozong of Tang</a> also proposed a Great Commandery of Gyerim for Silla, which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munmu-of-silla">Munmu of Silla</a> refused. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Emperor Taizong of Tang</a> had promised Silla Baekje and the lands south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> in return for its help, but he died before Goguryeo fell and Gaozong broke the promise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The people of Goguryeo revolted in 669, and the Tang deported tens of thousands of households to empty lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and Huai rivers; most sources give between 28,200 and 38,300 households. From 670 to 675 Goguryeo loyalists allied with Silla, among them Go Yeonmu and Geom Mojam, fought the Tang inside and outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, and in 676 Silla took all the land south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong River</a>. The seat was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Liaoyang</a> in 676 and to Xincheng, in modern Fushun, in 677. The deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bojang-of-goguryeo">Bojang of Goguryeo</a>, made commander there, plotted with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe people</a> to revive his kingdom and was banished in 681.</p><p>After the rebellion of the Khitan Li Jinzhong in 697, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was reduced in 698 to the Andong Area Command, and in 699 Tang troops left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>. Its rank was restored in 705, when the seat moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a>, west of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>; it moved again to Pingzhou in 714 and to the old city of Liaoxi in 743. In 736 the Tang formally recognised Silla's rule south of the Taedong. Sources date its end to 756, 758 or 761, when, according to the Old Book of Tang, it held only 5,718 households.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>According to Kim Jong-bok, neither Silla nor Parhae occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> after the Tang abandoned it. Silla did not advance beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong River</a>, and in 827 it mobilised 10,000 people to build a wall along the river against Parhae's attacks. Kim argues that Liaodong became a buffer zone between the Tang and Parhae, which explains the relative peace among the Tang, Parhae and Silla in contrast with the Goguryeo period; Parhae responded only in 924, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Liaoyang</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Protectorate General to Pacify the East” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tang set up the <b>Protectorate General to Pacify the East</b> there and deported tens of thousands of households to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
